<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/004A">Construction <span data-tag="004A">1.4.5.1</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. For every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, we let $\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}(X,Y)$ denote the set of homotopy classes of morphisms from $X$ to $Y$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. For every morphism $f: X \rightarrow Y$, we let $[f]$ denote its equivalence class in $\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} }(X,Y)$. </p>
<p>It follows from Propositions <a data-tag="0043" href="/tag/0043">1.4.4.2</a> and <a data-tag="0048" href="/tag/0048">1.4.4.7</a> that, for every triple of objects $X,Y, Z \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, there is a unique composition law </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \circ : \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( Y, Z) \times \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} }(X, Y) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( X, Z) \]
</div>
<p> satisfying the identity $[g] \circ [f] = [h]$ whenever $h: X \rightarrow Z$ is a composition of $f$ and $g$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. </p>
</article>
